## Auto-Controlled Fabrication of Metal-Porphyrin Framework Thin Film with Tunable Optical Limiting Effect
DOI: 10.1039/x0xx00000x www.rsc.org/
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with third-order nonlinear optical (NLO) properties are still in their infancy but very important. In this work, we first develop a layer by layer autoarm immersion method for preparing porphyrin-based MOF (PIZA-1) thin films with third-order NLO properties. By precisely controlling the thickness, the nonlinear adsorption of PIZA1 thin films can be switched continuously between reverse saturable absorption (RSA) and saturable absorption (SA) by using Z-scan technique. In addition, the optical limiting effect could be further optimized by loading ${\mathsf C}_{60}$ in the pores of PIZA-1 thin film. These findings not only open a new route for the exploitation of third-order NLO thin film materials, but also offer an insightful understanding of porphyrin-based MOF thin film for future broad practical applications.
## Introduction
The third-order nonlinear optical (NLO) materials have attracted much attention due to their potential applications in optical switching, optical limiting, logic devices, image transmission as well as mode-locked laser system.1-5 Thus, design and synthesis of new third-order NLO materials are important in modern chemistry and material science. The NLO behavior mainly includes reverse saturable absorption (RSA), saturable absorption (SA), nonlinear scattering (NLS) and nonlinear refraction (NLR), etc. So far, various organic/inorganic and hybrid materials have been synthesized for third-order NLO, including carbon nanodots, semiconductor quantum dots, conjugated organic molecules (porphyrins, phthalocyanines) or polymers, black phosphorus, metal-oxo clusters and so on.6-11 As a kind of porous crystalline hybrid materials, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)12-15 are constructed by the coordination of metal nodes (clusters) with organic linkers, which are supposed to be promising candidates to exploit optical properties including NLO, upconversion luminescence, lasing and so on.16-20 A few cases of powder MOFs have been realized to investigate the third-order NLO property.21-23 However, such powder MOFs have large scattering and usually are required to be dispersed in solvents for investigating the third-order NLO. Thus it is difficult to avoid the influence from solvents. So far, MOFs with the form of thin film have not been studied in third-order NLO property, but it’s important for practical applications.
Recently, MOF thin films have been used for various applications.24-28 Particularly, liquid-phase epitaxial (LPE) layer by layer method has been widely used for preparation of MOF thin film on substrate surfaces (also called SURMOFs)29-32 due to its controllable thicknesses, homogeneous surface, compact film and controllable growth orientation as well as effectively loading functional guest into MOF pores.33-36 Such high-quality MOF thin films will offer an opportunity for tuning third-order NLO performance and enhancing practical device application, such as pulse compression, Q-switching, mode-locking and optical limiters. In addition, as a special subclass of MOFs, metal-porphyrin frameworks (also called porphyrin-based MOFs) have interesting properties in catalysis, conductivity and photo-electric effect.37-41 Moreover, highly π-conjugated porphyrin based materials are wellknown to exhibit good third-order NLO response, particularly optical limiting effect.42, 43 Therefore, the porphyrin-based MOF thin films may be promising material for third-order NLO, but no reports are known to date.
