Herein, we develop a universal layer by layer autoarm immersion method with heating for the preparation of MOF thin films. This method is very labor-saving and cost-effective, which can be widely applied to prepare MOF thin films (such as many classic MOFs HKUST-1, MOF-2 and ${\mathsf C}\mathsf{o}_{2}(\mathsf{B D C})_{2}\mathsf{T E D}_{\cdot}$ , PIZA-1, etc). The autoarm immersion setup and preparation process of MOF and guest loaded MOF thin films are shown in Fig. S1. Notability, as a porphrin-based MOF, high quality PIZA-1 thin film provides an idea candidate for studying the third-order NLO properties. Here we firstly prepare porphyrin-based MOF PIZA-1 thin film on the quartz glass for study by tuning the thickness and loading fullerene molecules in the pores of PIZA-1 thin film (Fig. 1). Such PIZA-1 thin film with different thickness indicated the nonlinear absorption from reverse saturable absorption (RSA) to saturable absorption (SA) and the optical limiting effect could be further improved by loading ${\sf C}_{60}$ molecules. The superior optical limiting effect of ${\sf C}_{60}$ loaded PIZA-1 thin film may be attribute to the accumulation effect of energy transfer/photoinduced electron transfer (ET/PET) between porphyrin group and ${\mathsf C}_{60}$ , RSA, nonlinear scattering (NLS), and nonlinear refraction (NLR). Density functional theory (DFT) calculations reveal that the ET/PET derived from electrondonor/electron-acceptor interactions can enhance RSA behavior, which will optimize the optical limiting effect.
## Results and Discussion
Fig. 2a,b showed the structure and X-ray diffraction pattern of the PIZA-1 thin film, respectively. Two obvious XRD peaks of PIZA-1 thin film located at 6.1 and 12.2 were observed, indicating a [110]- orientation thin film.44 To study the thickness-dependent NLO behavior, the PIZA-1 thin films with 5, 10, 15, 20, and 25 cycles were grown on the quartz glass under the same conditions. The SEM images (Fig. 2c) of PIZA-1 thin film showed a homogeneous surface and continuous thin film as well as the average domain width size of $1.4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. S2a). Moreover, the PIZA-1 thin film has a low surface roughness with $\sim10\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. S2b) and the thicknesses of PIZA-1 thin films (Fig. S2c) were $\sim130$ , ${\sim}230$ , $\sim300$ , $\sim360$ and ${\sim}420$ nm for 5, 10, 15, 20 and 25 cycles (named PIZA-1-n, n is the LPE cycles), showing an approximate linear dependent on the LPE cycles (Fig. 2d). It illustrates PIZA-1 thin film can be prepared successfully and the thickness can be well controlled by the layer by layer process. In addition, the transmittance of PIZA-1 thin film with different thickness decreases with increasing thickness (Fig. 2e, f) showing an approximate exponential decay to LPE cycles and the absorbance of PIZA-1 thin film with different cycles exhibit an approximate exponential increase on the LPE cycles. After fitting the data, this points were identical to the fitting nonlinear curve according to the equation $\mathsf{A}=2\mathsf{-}\mathsf{l o g}(\mathsf{T})$ . In order to show the universal thin film preparation method, MOFs HKUST-1, MOF-2 and ${\mathsf C}_{0_{2}}({\mathsf B}{\mathsf D}{\mathsf C})_{2}\mathsf{T}{\mathsf E}{\mathsf D}$ thin films are successfully prepared in this work, which can be demonstrated by the XRD and SEM characterizaiton in Fig. S3-5.
The NLO properties of PIZA-1 thin film with different thickness grown on the quartz glass (Fig. 3b) are studied by a typical open and close aperture Z-scan system (Fig. 3a) with a nanosecond laser at $532~\mathsf{n m}$ . As shown in Fig. $3\mathsf{c}^{\sim}\mathsf{g},$ PIZA-1-5 and PIZA-1-10 thin films exhibit a typical RSA response and the RSA response increases with the increase of the incident pulse energy. The minimum normalized transmittances $(\mathsf{T}_{\mathsf{m i n}})$ at $Z=0$ are about 1 $(30~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ , 0.96 $(50~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ , 0.81 $(80~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ and 0.74 $(100~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ for PIZA-1-5, and 0.87 $(30~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ , 0.78 $(50~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ , 0.70 $(30~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ and 0.65 $(100~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ for PIZA-1-10, suggesting that PIZA-1- 5 and PIZA-1-10 have a good RSA response with optical limiting effect at $532\ \mathsf{n m}$ .  PIZA-1-15 and PIZA-1-20 thin films show RSA response at low pulse energy (30 and $50~\upmu\mathrm{J})$ ) firstly and then display a switching behavior from RSA to SA response after ${\sim}80~\upmu\mathrm{{J}}$ . The normalized transmittance of PIZA-1-25 gradually increases and this SA behavior could enable to be developed as a passive mode locker or Q-switcher for ultrafast laser pulse generation.
The nonlinear absorption coefficient $\beta$ were calculated by fitting the Z-scan curves from Fig. 3c,d,g and shown in Table S1 and Fig. S6. The negative value represents the SA response while the positive value corresponds to the RSA response.45 The increase of $\beta$ with incident pulse energy implies that the nonlinear absorption is also influenced by nonlinear scattering in the high-fluence regime.46 To compare the NLO behavior of PIZA-1 thin film with different thickness clearly, a comparison of the results under the same incident pulse energy of $100~\upmu\mathrm{J}$ was shown in Fig. 3h and S7-8. The switching from RSA to SA with increasing the thickness was clearly observed. For the PIZA-1-15 and PIZA-1-20, the RSA-SA-RSA switching behavior can be attributed to the dominant excited state absorption at lower intensities (far from the laser focus) and saturation of excited states at higher intensity (near the laser focus).47 Furthermore, the UV-vis absorbance spectra (Fig. S9) showed the absorbance of PIZA-1 thin film increased with increasing the thicknesses. The band gaps of PIZA-1 thin film with different thickness (Fig. S10) was calcuated to be same with ${\sim}1.73$ eV. Therefore, the thickness dependence of NLO behavior can be attributed to the transmittance decrease of PIZA-1 thin film with increasing the thicknesses. The behavior resulted in the nonlinear absorption of PIZA-1 thin film was changed from RSA to SA when tuning the thickness.
Another effective approach for tuning the NLO response is introducing the guest species into MOF thin films. Herein an effective enhancing the NLO property is introducing ${\mathsf{C}}_{60}$ into the thin film due to its good RSA behavior. The ${\sf C}_{60}$ loaded PIZA-1 $(C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-}$ 1) thin film with the 10 cycles on the quartz glass are prepared successfully by the modified autoarm immersion layer by layer approach. The successful loading of ${\sf C}_{60}$ in PIZA-1 thin film was proved by the characterizations such as the quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) (Fig. S11), UV-vis absorption spectrum (Fig. S12), photocurrent response (Fig. S13) and the current–voltage curve (Fig. S14). For comparison, all experiments were carried out under the same treatment. The water uptakes of PIZA-1 and $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film in QCM test were calculated to be $4.94\:\mathsf{m g}\:\mathsf{c m^{-}}$ 2 and $1.91\mathrm{\mg\cm^{-2}}$ , demonstrating that ${\sf C}_{60}$ molecules were loaded in the pores successfully. The absorption bands at $330\textmu m_{\mathrm{e}}$ Ownelirne observed in the UV-vis absorption spectrumDoOf :C160.@10P3I9Z/AC-91StCh0i5n8f8il1mH and the enhanced photocurrent property also indicated that ${\sf C}_{60}$ was encapsulated into PIZA-1 thin film. This introduction of ${\sf C}_{60}$ could increase the separation efficiency of photogenerated electrons and holes, resulting in the improved photoactivity. Besides, embedding ${\sf C}_{60}$ in the PIZA-1 thin film increases the electrical conductivity compared with the PIZA-1 thin film (Fig. S14). On the other hand, the stability of $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film was verified by the XRD, IR and SEM. The XRD data (Fig. S15) of $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film reveal that the diffraction pattern did not change before and after loading. Moreover, the IR spectra (Fig. S16) and SEM images (Fig. S17) of ${\mathsf{C}}_{60}$ loaded PIZA-1 thin film was similar to that of pristine thin film. The IR spectra bands at the range of 1390–1700 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ corresponded to the vibrational stretching of carboxylate groups and pyrrole groups. Due to the IR bands of ${\sf C}_{60}$ were overlapped with PIZA-1 IR spectra, the ${\mathsf C}_{60}@{\mathsf P}|\boldsymbol{Z}{\mathsf A}-1$ don’t show the appearance peak of ${\mathsf{C}}_{60}$ .
Fullerenes (C60), as a class of carbon structurVei,ewhAartvicele Obnelienne considered as favorable electron accepDtOoIr:s1,0.10m3e9a/nC9wShCil0e588t1hHe porphyrin serves as an electron donor. The observed luminescence quenching (Fig. S22) indicates that there is a strong interaction between the excited state of PIZA-1 and fullerenes moieties in $\mathsf C_{60}@\mathsf P|\mathsf Z\mathsf A-1$ thin film. Possible pathways for the fluorescence quenching of $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film may be attributed to two possible competitive processes: photoinduced electron transfer (PET) and energy transfer (ET). The efficient ET/PET process in several porphyrin–carbon structure systems has been reported.50-53 The photoinduced electron transfer can be evidenced by the photocurrent responses and the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements (Fig. S13, S23). Regarding to the donor–acceptor structure and fluorescence quenching behavior, the ET/PET from the electron donor PIZA-1 to the acceptor fullerenes plays an important role for enhancing optical limiting effect. In addition, RSA, NLS and NLR from porphyrin and ${\sf C}_{60}$ should be responsible for the third-order NLO response of $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film. Thanks to the accumulation effect of RSA, NLS, NLR and ET/PET process, the $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film exhibited superior NLO performance to the individual component.
To gain further insight into the observed electron-donor /electron-acceptor interactions in the $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film, the theoretical calculation is carried out based on density functional theory (DFT).The optimized periodic structures and the results of electronic density of states (DOS) calculations for PIZA-1 and $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film are shown in Fig. 5. Compared with the PIZA-1, the valence band maximum (VBM) of the $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ is contributed by the PIZA-1, while the conduction band minimum (CBM) originate from the ${\mathsf{C}}_{60}$ moiety. Besides, the calculated nominal band gap of the $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film decreases by 0.66 eV comparing with the PIZA-1 thin film, indicating the excited electrons easily transfer from the porphyrin to ${\sf C}_{60}$ moiety in the presence of laser light. The band gap of $\mathsf C_{60}@\mathsf P\mathsf{I Z A}-1$ thin film was comparable or smaller than those of many reported porphyrin-fullerene systems (Table S2). The emergence of electron transfer herein is thought to be responsible for the enhancement of the third-order NLO properties. The combination of PIZA-1 with ${\sf C}_{60}$ results in the improvement of the charge separation and transport capability between porphyrin groups and ${\sf C}_{60}$ moiety, which consist with the results of photocurrent response and electrical conductivity. Resultantly, the structure of $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ with high density of donor–acceptor interfaces had enhanced optical limiting response.
## Conclusions
In summary, we have reported a layer by layer autoarm immersion method for preparing MOF thin films with tunable thickness, homogeneous surface and effective guest loading. The third-order nonlinear absorption of PIZA-1 thin films was switched continuously between RSA and SA by tuning the thickness. Moreover, the optical limiting effect was improved by loading ${\sf C}_{60}$ in the pores of PIZA-1 thin film. The electronic DOS calculations demonstrated that the enhanced optical limiting effect of $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film was mainly attributed to the energy transfer/photoinduced electron transfer (ET/PET) between porphyrin groups and fullerenes. Taken as a whole, a promising thin film material for optical limiting is reported and it provides an insightful understanding of porphyrin-based MOF thin film for future  broad optical applications in pulse compression, Q-switching, mode-locking and optical limiters.
