## Experimental
Materials and instrumentation. All of the chemicals were used after purchasing without further purification. The quartz glass and Si subatrate were first cleaned using distilled water in an ultrasonic bath and dried before activation. And then they was treated with a mixture of ${0.2\ \mathsf{m M}\ \mathsf{N a O H}}$ aqueous solution and hydrogen peroxide $(30\%)$ with a volume ratio 3:1 at $80^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 30 mins and then cleaned with deionized water and dried under nitrogen flux for the next preparation. The Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis were performed on a MiniFlex2 X-ray diffractometer using $c_{u-1}\leq a$ radiation $(\lambda=0.1542\ \mathsf{n m})$ ) in the 2θ range of $5-30^{\circ}$ with a scanning rate of $0.5^{\circ}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ . IRRAS data were recorded using a Bruker Vertex 70 FTIR spectrometer with $2\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ resolution at an angle of incidence of $80^{\circ}$ relative to the surface normal. Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images for the morphology of thin films were measured by JSM6700. The AFM images were recorded with a Bruker Dimension ICON. The thinckness was measured by stylus profiler (Bruker DEKTAK XT). The UV-vis spectra for the samples were measured by Lambda 365. The photoluminescent spectra were measured by using Edinburgh Instrument FLS920. Photocurrent response was carried out by the CHI760e electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chenhua Instrument China).
Fabrication of PIZA-1 thin film with different thickness. The PIZA-1 thin film used in the present work were grown using the layer-bylayer autoarm immersion method and were fabricated using the following diluted ethanolic solutions at room tempeature: cobalt acetate $(1.0~\mathsf{m M})$ TCPP (5,10,15,20-(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin, $0.1~\mathsf{m M};$ ). The detail was shown as follow: fristly, the functionalizd quartz glass was immersed in the solution of cobalt acetate for 10 min and then was immersed in solution of TCPP for 15 min. Each step was washed with pure ethanol to remove residual reactants. The above detail belong to one cycle. A total of 5, 10, 15, 20, and 25 growth cycles were used for PIZA-1 thin film grown on quartz glass.
Fabrication of $\pmb{C}_{60}$ Loaded PIZA-1 Thin Films. The $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film were fabricated by the layer-by-layer autoarm immersion method. The substrate was immersed in cobalt acetate $(1.0~\mathsf{m M})$ , TCPP (5,10,15,20-(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin, $0.1\mathsf{m M}^{\prime}$ and ${\sf C}_{60}$ (0.2 mM) solutions sequentially. The immersed times were 10, 15, and 5 mins for ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{O A c}})_{2},$ TCPP ethnaol solution and ${\sf C}_{60}$ toluene solution, respectively. Each step was washed with pure ethanol to remove residual reactants. A total of 10 growth cycles were used for ${\sf C}_{60}$ encapsulation in PIZA-1 thin flm in this work.
Fabrication of HKUST-1 thin film, MOF-2 thin film and $\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{B D C})_{2}\mathsf{T E D}$ thin film. The method of preparing HKUST-1 thin film and MOF-2 thin film were same as the fabrication of PIZA-1 thin film, except for the type of metal and linker solution. For the HKUST-1 thin film, the metal and linker solution are copper acetate $(1~\mathsf{m M})$ , BTC (1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid, $0.4~\mathsf{m M})$ , respectively. The metal and linker solution are copper acetate $(1\ m\mathsf{M})$ and ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid, $0.4~\mathsf{m M}$ ) for the MOF-2 thin film. The ${\mathsf C}\circ_{2}({\mathsf B}{\mathsf D}{\mathsf C})_{2}\mathsf T{E}{\mathsf D}$ thin film was prepared at $50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ .The metal and linker solution are cobalt acetate $(1\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M})$ and the mixed solution of ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{B D C}}$ (1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid, $0.4m\mathsf{M})$ and TED (Triethylenediamine, $0.4\mathsf{m}\mathsf{M}$ ) , respectively.
Fabrication of porphyrin ligand, $\pmb{C}_{60}$ and the porphyrin ligand mixed with $\pmb{C}_{60}$ film on quartz glass. 0.1mM TCPP (5,10,15,20-(4- carboxyphenyl) porphyrin) ethanol solution was coated on quartz glass using a drop-coating method. After the solvent evaporates at room temperature, the TCPP ethanol solution was recoated on quartz glass again. The above operation was repeatedViseewvAertricalletOi nmlienes until the samples were adjusted to keeDpOI:a1n0 1i0d3e9/nCti9cSaCl 0l5i8n8e1aHr transmittance of $\sim85\%$ . The preparation method of ${\sf C}_{60}$ and the porphyrin ligand mixed with ${\sf C}_{60}$ films on quartz glass were similar to the fabrication of porphyrin ligand film on quartz glass.
Electrochemical Measurements. The photocurrent tests and the (EIS) were carried out in the CHI760e electrochemical workstation (Shanghai Chenhua Instrument China) using a three electrode electrochemical mode in 0.2 M ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S O}}_{4}$ aqueous solution at room temperature where Pt wire served as the counter electrode and ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ as the reference electrode, the PIZA-1thin film and $\mathsf C_{60}@\mathsf P|\mathsf Z\mathsf A-1$ thin film grown FTO were directly selected as the working electrode without any ancillary materials (electrode size of $1\times1~\mathsf{c m}^{2},$ . The photocurrent responses of the samples were recorded with voltage bias of $\textsf{0v}$ and the working electrode was illuminated by a 300 W Xe lamp (Beijing Perfect Light, PLS-SXE300C). The EIS measurements were carried out at an open-circuit voltage in a frequency range from $0.1{\mathsf{H z}}$ to $100~\mathsf{k H z}$ with an amplitude of 5 $\mathsf{m}\mathsf{v}$ and the working electrode was illuminated with $532{\mathsf{n m}}$ laser.
Quartz crystal microbalance (QCM) Measurements. A commercial QCM 200 with a flow module for measurements in gas phase was adopted to monitor the mass uptake and adsorption rate of present PIZA-1 thin film and $C_{60}@\mathsf{P l Z A-1}$ thin film with 10 cycles, which were prepared on MUD SAMs functionalized QCM working electrodes. The QCM can be used in gas phase environments for monitoring the change of mass by monitoring the resonance frequency changes on the electrode thin film. In this work, the water was chosen as the loading analytes.
Z-scan Measurements. The nonlinear optical property of the sample was evaluated using the Z-scan technique. The excitation light source was an Nd:YAG laser with a repetition rate of $^{10\ \mathsf{H z}}$ . The laser pulses (period, 5 ns; wavelength, $532~\mathsf{n m}$ ) were split into two beams with a mirror. The pulse energies at the front and back of the samples were monitored using energy detectors 1 and 2. All of the measurements were conducted at room temperature. The sample was mounted on a computer-controlled translation stage that shifted each sample along the z-axis.
The measured z-scan curves were fitted by the following expression:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{T(Z,S=1)=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}(Z,0)}{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}}L n\big[1+q_{0}(Z,0)e^{-r^{2}}\big]d r}\end{array}
$$
$$
q_{0}(Z,0)=\beta I_{0}L_{e f f}
$$
$$
L_{e f f}=\frac{1-e^{-\alpha l}}{\alpha}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{T(Z,\Delta\phi)=1+\frac{4\Delta\phi\mathrm{x}}{(\mathrm{x}^{2}+9)(\mathrm{x}^{2}+1)}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\chi\{^{3}\}=\frac{2\varepsilon_{0}c^{2}n_{0}^{2}}{3\upomega}\beta
$$
$$
\chi_{R}^{(3)}={\frac{4c n_{0}^{2}\varepsilon_{0}}{3}}n_{2}
$$
$$
|\chi^{(3)}|=\sqrt{\left(\chi_{R}^{(3)}\right)^{2}+\left(\chi_{I}^{(3)}\right)^{2}}
$$
In these equations, $I_{O}$ is the on-axis peak intensity at the focus (Z $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=0_{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}^{\prime}$ ), $L_{e f f}$ is the effective thickness of the sample, $\alpha_{0}$ is the linear absorption coefficient, and $I$ is the sample thickness, $\mathsf{x}=\mathsf{z}/\mathsf{z}_{0},\mathsf{z}$ is the Z-scan displacement, $\Delta\phi$ is the phase change, $\scriptstyle\varepsilon_{O}$ is the permittivity of vacuum, $c$ is the speed of light, $n_{O}$ is the refractive index of the medium $,\omega=2\pi c/\lambda,n_{2}(e s u)=\frac{c n_{0}}{40\pi}\gamma(m^{2}/W).$ By fitting the curves, the nonlinear absorption coefficient $\beta,$ the nonlinear refractive coefficient $\gamma$ and the third-order nonlinear susceptibilities $\chi^{(3)}$ were obtained.
